course: dictionary
lesson: 4016
title: Garner: gathering and gaining
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Garner is a formal word, so you will meet it more in news, essays and old books than in everyday talk. Its main meaning is to collect or gain something, such as support, praise, votes or information. «The idea garnered support» means that more and more people started to back the idea.
explain: The words around garner give you clues. In the Rat's picnic, the flask holds «sunshine shed and garnered on far Southern slopes». Sunshine cannot be bought in a shop, so garnered must mean gathered in, collected from the sunny hillsides over time. Check it by swapping in your meaning: "sunshine gathered on far Southern slopes" still makes sense.
explain: Everyday speech uses get or gather instead of garner, so when you see it in a book, try those words first. In very old texts, garner can also be a noun: a garner is a building for storing grain. A dictionary helps you see which meaning fits.
fact: Garner means to collect or gain something, such as support, praise, votes or information.
fact: «The film garnered praise» means the film gained lots of good comments from people.
fact: In old writing, to garner can mean to gather and store grain, and a garner is a building where grain is kept.
fact: In the Rat's picnic, «bottled sunshine» is a joke for wine: the grapes soaked up the sun on sunny southern hills.
fact: Garner sounds bookish, so you can often swap in get or gather and the sentence will still make sense.
exercise: The new park garnered praise from everyone who visited. What does garnered mean here? | Cleaned up | Lost forever | *Collected or gained | Sold cheaply
exercise: The Rat's flask held «sunshine shed and garnered on far Southern slopes». What does garnered mean? | Thrown away | *Gathered in | Frozen solid | Dropped on the floor
exercise: Which everyday word could replace garner in «She garnered votes for the school council»? | forget | *get | sleep | jump
test: In old writing, a garner is what? | A small bird that sings | *A building for storing grain | A kind of garden gnome | A ship that sails the sea
test: «The idea garnered support.» What did the idea gain? | *Support from people | A lost tooth | A new colour | A long holiday
exam: Which is the best meaning of garner in «Her book garnered great reviews»? | *Gained or collected | Read aloud softly | Hidden under a bed | Torn into pieces
